{
  "metadata": {
    "name": "Italian phoneme inventory",
    "notes": "Italian (standard, Tuscan-based) phoneme inventory on the 27 features of romanian_features.json, in the same order. Segments shared with Romanian (i u a p b t d k ɡ t͡s t͡ʃ d͡ʒ f v s z ʃ ʒ m n l r j w) copy its values exactly; Romanian ɨ ə h are absent (h is a silent letter in Italian). Deviations: e and o are Tense + (close-mid), and the new open-mid ɛ and ɔ are e and o with Tense -; the e/ɛ and o/ɔ contrast holds only under stress, a distributional fact the inventory does not encode. Tense stays 0 on i u a, as in Romanian. New consonants: d͡z (t͡s with Voice +, lexical: zero, zona), ɲ and ʎ (n and l with Anterior - and Distributed +, coded like the postalveolars ʃ t͡ʃ d͡ʒ: coronal place, DORSAL -, tongue-body features 0). Geminates are single segments: each geminable consonant has a twin labelled with the length mark (kː, t͡ʃː, ʃː …) that differs only in Long +; z j w (and marginal ʒ, found in loans) have no geminate. Wiktionary writes geminates across the syllable dot (ˈsak.ko, ˈfat.t͡ʃa); the Italian analysis merges them into the long segment before tokenizing, and its tokenizer attaches ː to the preceding segment. [ŋ] and [ɱ] are allophones of n and are not listed. Conventions as in Romanian: velars are DORSAL + with the tongue-body features High/Low/Front/Back/Round 0 (reserved for vocoids); DelRel is - on oral stops, + on affricates, 0 elsewhere; Stress is - throughout, because stress is not segmental here (the tokenizer drops ˈ and ˌ; the analysis reads stress from the transcription). The underspecified dorsals (/K G/ and their geminate and sc-cluster twins) and the analysis patches live in phonology.analyses.italian_palatalization, not here; Italian has no underspecified coronals. Sources and justifications: docs/italian/phonology.md."
  },
